WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.933
Inside the model.

2
00:00:02.233 --> 00:00:06.089
Twelve terms for architecture internals and generation controls.

3
00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:10.667
These are the words you hear in every how does it actually run conversation.

4
00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:15.727
The KV cache stores the keys and values from earlier tokens,

5
00:00:15.727 --> 00:00:20.167
so each new token doesn't recompute attention over the whole history.

6
00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:23.151
That's why long chats eat GPU memory,

7
00:00:23.151 --> 00:00:27.867
and it's what vLLM's PagedAttention manages, like virtual memory.

8
00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:30.633
Inference has two phases.

9
00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:34.700
Prefill reads your whole prompt in parallel, and it is compute-bound.

10
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.047
Decode generates one token at a time, and it is memory-bound.

11
00:00:39.047 --> 00:00:41.967
Serving optimizations treat them separately.

12
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:49.700
RoPE, rotary positional embedding, encodes a token's position by rotating its query and key vectors.

13
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.123
It's the modern default in Llama, Qwen, and Mistral, and its scaling tricks,

14
00:00:55.123 --> 00:00:59.033
YaRN and NTK-aware, are how context windows got stretched.

15
00:01:00.233 --> 00:01:06.000
ALiBi skips position embeddings entirely, and instead penalizes attention by distance.

16
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.222
It's simpler than RoPE and extrapolates to longer sequences.

17
00:01:10.222 --> 00:01:12.967
You'll see it in MPT and BLOOM era models.

18
00:01:14.167 --> 00:01:21.233
FlashAttention is an exact attention algorithm, reorganized to minimize slow GPU memory reads.

19
00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:25.133
Same math, two to four times faster, and far less memory.

20
00:01:25.433 --> 00:01:30.900
Versions two and three refine it, and it sits under nearly every modern training and serving stack.

21
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:38.367
Grouped-query and multi-query attention let several query heads share a single key and value head.

22
00:01:38.667 --> 00:01:42.567
That shrinks the KV cache dramatically, with minimal quality loss.

23
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:45.533
It's why Llama-3-class models serve cheaply.

24
00:01:46.733 --> 00:01:52.533
With sliding-window attention, each token looks only at the last N tokens instead of everything.

25
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:55.266
Memory stays constant as context grows.

26
00:01:55.266 --> 00:01:57.200
It's Mistral's signature trick.

27
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.268
A mixture of experts has many parallel feed-forward experts,

28
00:02:02.268 --> 00:02:05.233
with a router activating only a few per token.

29
00:02:05.533 --> 00:02:10.000
So you get a huge model that only pays for a fraction of it on each inference.

30
00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.408
Mixtral runs two of its eight experts per token,

31
00:02:13.408 --> 00:02:19.300
DeepSeek-V3 runs eight of two hundred fifty six, and reportedly frontier models use it too.

32
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.400
State space models are an attention alternative.

33
00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:27.416
They process sequences in linear time with constant memory.

34
00:02:27.416 --> 00:02:32.267
Mamba is the flagship, and hybrids like Jamba mix them with attention layers.

35
00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:35.467
Watch this space, but don't bet your career on it yet.

36
00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:40.739
RWKV and RetNet are other attention-free architectures,

37
00:02:40.739 --> 00:02:45.033
RNN flavored, with transformer-level training parallelism.

38
00:02:45.333 --> 00:02:47.668
Same pitch as Mamba: linear scaling.

39
00:02:47.668 --> 00:02:50.133
Recognition-level knowledge is enough.

40
00:02:51.333 --> 00:02:55.177
The model's raw output is one score per vocabulary token.

41
00:02:55.177 --> 00:02:57.267
Those scores are called logits.

42
00:02:57.567 --> 00:02:59.733
Softmax turns them into probabilities.

43
00:03:00.033 --> 00:03:03.933
Every sampling knob you'll meet next operates on this distribution.

44
00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:09.867
Temperature below one sharpens the distribution, above one flattens it.

45
00:03:09.867 --> 00:03:16.000
Top-k keeps the k likeliest tokens, and top-p keeps the smallest set covering probability p.

46
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:21.367
Temperature zero is roughly deterministic, the setting for extraction and evals.

47
00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:23.533
Let's recap.

48
00:03:23.833 --> 00:03:29.233
The KV cache, plus GQA and FlashAttention, keep memory and speed under control.

49
00:03:29.533 --> 00:03:33.652
RoPE, ALiBi, and sliding windows handle position and length.

50
00:03:33.652 --> 00:03:37.633
MoE and state space models change the architecture itself.

51
00:03:37.933 --> 00:03:40.267
And logits feed every sampling control.
